{"id": "d73e7f3c-31c8-49c4-b91a-1d424c6cedc7", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I'm finding that the S3 Gateway Endpoint is only associated with the route table for the private subnet in us-east-1a, but not the one for the second private subnet — a gap that could cause routing issues for resources in that other subnet.

This confirms the root cause: Private Subnet B's route table lacks the S3 prefix-list route, so any workloads there send S3 traffic through 0.0.0.0/0 to the NAT Gateway instead of the endpoint. I'm now planning to validate this against flow logs by checking traffic on the NAT ENI for S3 prefix-list destinations and matching source addresses to that subnet.

"}], "parent_id": "2cccac1d-cb06-4199-ba0f-4cc6d3978ba2"}

{"id": "88bd00c3-eac9-49ee-bd38-a94b77d8f8fd", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I'm realizing describe_managed_prefix_lists is a regional call, so it only returns the managed prefix lists (like S3/DynamoDB) that belong to the queried region itself.

That means verifying whether a destination belongs to another region's S3 prefix list requires repeating the query per region. Rather than checking all regions, I'm examining the destination IP ranges themselves — many like 44.x, 35.x, 13.217.x, 98.87.x, and 100.54.x look like general EC2 ranges tied to us-east-1 rather than S3-specific ranges, suggesting this traffic may not actually be S3-bound at all.

"}], "parent_id": "2cccac1d-cb06-4199-ba0f-4cc6d3978ba2"}
